  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Nolkup: An App to Access Low Arsenic Water in Bangladesh.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Toxic levels of arsenic contaminate one-third of tubewells in rural Bangladesh and affect the drinking water of up to 100 million people. An estimated 100,000 annual deaths in Bangladesh have been linked to chronic arsenic exposure from drinking well-water. To help families know which wells are contaminated, Columbia University developed the Nolkup (tubewell in Bangla) smartphone app. A tubewell is a common type of water well in Bangladesh. The location-based app tells the user the arsenic content of tested wells nearby so they can make informed decisions about where to obtain water. Traditionally, environmental information is communicated via costly and lengthy door-to-door information campaigns, if at all. Nolkup is a convenient and cost-effective method of delivering public information on arsenic levels. With support from Development Innovation Ventures, Columbia University is running a randomized controlled trial to test whether the technology can have positive effects on behaviors and decision-making but more quickly and at lower cost. The Bangladesh governments Department of Public Health Engineering is responsible for testing wells under its Arsenic Risk Reduction Program and has agreed to make five to ten million well test results available on Nolkup. Columbia University is testing the app in partnership with the NGO Forum and Innovations for Poverty Action Bangladesh. By testing the effectiveness of app-based water quality information services, Columbia University and its partners will measure if this low-cost solution leads to higher utilization of clean drinking water. That in turn will inform effective ways to leverage the data being collected by the government on five to ten million wells.</narrative>
  </description>
